experiment  bn_decomposition: separate alpha_a from alpha_b, and test whether the model is complete

## Predictions, registered before anything is measured

THE ARITHMETIC (check it, do not test it; it is algebra, not a claim)
  W = 3 F H = 176,160,768 elements per expert; one M-tile carries 2H + 3F = 51,200 elements per row read or written once,
  and W/BN re-read per extra N-tile. K = W/(2H+3F) = 3440.6 (ai_model.py uses the up-GEMM's K = 4096, a 16% difference,
  worth 0.0060 in alpha at BM=128).
  alpha_fitted = (alpha_b + phi)/(1 + phi + delta)   EXACT
  alpha_fitted = alpha_b + alpha_a (BM/BN) + BM/K    the study's form, the same thing linearised at small phi
  B/C = ridge b (alpha_b + phi) / (2 BM), scored at ridge = 152.81 Op/B (measured on this device: NVIDIA H200 (measured), 668.5 TFLOP/s bf16 over 4374.5 GB/s (triad pattern); calibrated 2026-09-09 at 981a42f6).

P1  alpha_a, as a SLOPE over three or more BN and not a two-point difference, lands in [0.10, 0.38].
    BASIS  alpha_a band [0.10, 0.38], derived from the ONLY pair of committed arms that differ in BLOCK_SIZE_N and nothing else:
             BM= 32  alpha 1.0073 (BN=64) - 0.9001 (BN=256) over ds 0.375  ->  alpha_a 0.286 +/- 0.086
             BM= 64  alpha 0.9327 (BN=64) - 0.8235 (BN=256) over ds 0.750  ->  alpha_a 0.146 +/- 0.043
             source: mixtral-8x7b-bf16-r1024-g1-n64-d66ad3.report.json vs mixtral-8x7b-bf16-r1024-g1-n256-16cc16.report.json
             every +/- above is the DECLARED prior 0.0229, times sqrt(2) for the difference of two arms, divided by that pair's ds. It is PINNED to the declared number and deliberately NOT re-read from a measured floor: a pre-registration that moves when new information arrives is not one, so this band and the C6 bar built on it stay where they were registered even after part (a) publishes. What a measured floor moves is what the run can RESOLVE, which is the MDE and the cross-arm floor printed beside every interval below.
             the band is 0.28 wide against input sds of 0.043-0.086, i.e. 3.2-6.5x the spread of the numbers that built it. A PASS inside it is weak evidence; a FAIL outside it is not. Narrowing it is what a three-point BN fit is for, which is this run.
    A FAIL at zero says BN does not move alpha and there is no activation re-read to model.
    A FAIL high, with a clean residual, says the two-point slopes were biased by the denominator.

P2  THE RESIDUAL IS NOISE: chi2 <= 4.0 against this run's own bootstrap sigma.
    This is the registered expectation and the one most likely to FAIL, because the published
    G=1 pair ALREADY disagrees with the model: read through (EXA), BN=64 implies alpha_b 0.92
    and BN=256 implies 0.81, a 0.107 gap against a published cross-arm alpha spread of 0.023.
    A FAIL names its shape: the candidate is alpha_b itself moving with BN, because BN changes
    the order the L2 is walked in -- a term the three-term model forbids.

P3  alpha_b is the SAME at every BLOCK_M, within 3 bootstrap sigma. Nothing in the model lets a
    weight miss fraction depend on the tile height, so this is the cheapest test of the whole
    decomposition and it needs no external number.

P4  alpha_b at GROUP_SIZE_M=16, against TEMPO's b2/b of 0.311/0.319. NO prior agreement is claimed:
    the study's earlier 'decomposed 0.307 corroborates TEMPO to 2-4%' is WITHDRAWN, because 0.307
    was ALPHA_BY_BLOCK_M solved through (LIN), a unit artefact of the estimator, and pooled over
    GROUP_SIZE_M 1, 8, 16 and 64 besides. This run pins ONE swizzle. Read through (EXA) the study's
    own G=1 ladders put alpha_b near 0.92, so C4 is predicted to FAIL at G=16 unless it is 16 or
    above -- and that failure is a statement about the swizzle, not about TEMPO. Either verdict is the
    first alpha_b in this study that may be compared with TEMPO at all.

P5  WHICH CELLS GO MISSING, registered because two of them are predicted to.
    THE PRIMARY AT BN=64 IS PREDICTED TO BE DISCARDED: the anchor is 0.991, inside the parallel-branch
    tolerance of 0.15, and that is the measured median of 22 published ladders rather than a guess.
    At BN=32 the primary is predicted to escape UP (every tread memory bound) and at BN=128 to keep a
    prefix, so it should contribute 2 of 3 points and the POOLED fit carries the residual test. BM=64
    and BM=32 sit far outside the band at every BN, which is what makes the sweep survive that.
    THE BN=32 ARM IS THE ONE AT RISK: its BLOCK_M=256 reference is predicted compute bound in the LADDER
    world and MEMORY bound in the LIN-ERA one, so the two worlds disagree about whether the arm exists.
    If it is refused only two BN values remain, V5 FAILS, alpha_a is UNIDENTIFIED, and the deliverable is
    that refusal plus this prediction naming it in advance -- not a fitted number.

P6  BLOCK_N=256 CANNOT BE MEASURED AT ALL on either card as pinned: BM=256 x BN=256 needs 256
    accumulator registers per thread against a maximum of 255, so the arm has no reference. It is
    excluded by arithmetic before any GPU time, and the resource bill below prints the number.

P7  The references agree across BN to within 2.0x. The failure this bar exists for is 43.6x,
    from a kernel that spilled its accumulator and still fitted a line through the origin to 0.2%.

